From f3cc7b86f877f3319af611aef68fbc0ac131c1e3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: kebab bring-up <local@invalid>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 11:50:13 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] kebab: front camera VANA gpio156 is active-high, not
 active-low

The IMX471 front-camera VANA load switch on gpio156 was declared
GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW by patch 0060. That is inverted.

Ground truth, from two independent copies of the vendor devicetree for
this exact board:

  kona-oem-camera-kebab_t0.dtsi:635   cam_sensor_front_avdd_suspend
  kebab-19805-camera.dts:14032        cam_sensor_front_avdd_suspend

both configure gpio156 as "output-low; bias-pull-down;" in the camera-off
(suspend) state, and plain "bias-disable" when active. Off is therefore
low and on is high: the rail is active-high.

With the inverted flag, gpiolib did the exact opposite of what was
intended. While the regulator was nominally disabled the pin was parked
high, leaving VANA on; and when imx471_power_on() called
regulator_bulk_enable() the pin was driven low, cutting VANA for the whole
duration of the chip-id read at 0x0016. Every probe therefore talked to a
sensor whose analog supply had just been removed, which is precisely the
reproducible

  imx471 23-001a: Error reading reg 0x0016: -6
  imx471 23-001a: error -ENXIO: failed to read chip id

signature. The live state agreed: with the regulator disabled,
/sys/kernel/debug/gpio showed "gpio156 : out high".

This also retroactively invalidates the two full address-space bus scans
(0070 and 0075) that were used to argue for a dead sensor. Both ran inside
imx471's powered window, i.e. inside the window where VANA was actually
off, so neither could ever have seen the sensor ACK. The module EEPROM
kept answering at 0x50/0x58 throughout because it is fed from pm8008_l4
(cam_vio, regulator-always-on), not from VANA -- which is exactly why the
bus looked electrically alive while the sensor looked dead.

Fix the flag and add the explicit enable-active-high property.
---
 .../boot/dts/qcom/sm8250-oneplus-kebab.dts    | 19 ++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8250-oneplus-kebab.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8250-oneplus-kebab.dts
index ad2068ad1..107bb1a45 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8250-oneplus-kebab.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8250-oneplus-kebab.dts
@@ -1971,6 +1971,22 @@ &cci1 {
  * dedicated GPIO (gpio-req-tbl-label = "CAM_front_VANA", raw pin 0x9c=156)
  * in addition to the shared vreg_bob rail -- modeled as a fixed regulator
  * so the imx471 driver's own "vana" regulator_bulk_enable() toggles it.
+ *
+ * Polarity is ACTIVE HIGH. The vendor's own pinctrl for this pin
+ * (kona-oem-camera-kebab_t0.dtsi, cam_sensor_front_avdd_suspend, and the
+ * same node in the extracted kebab-19805-camera.dts) drives gpio156
+ * "output-low; bias-pull-down;" in the *suspend* / camera-off state --
+ * therefore off == low and on == high.
+ *
+ * Patch 0060 previously declared this GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW, which inverted the
+ * rail: gpiolib parked the pin high (VANA on) while the regulator was
+ * nominally disabled, and drove it low (VANA off) exactly when
+ * imx471_power_on() enabled the supply -- i.e. the sensor's analog rail was
+ * cut for the duration of every chip-id read. That is the -ENXIO NACK, and
+ * it also invalidates both earlier full-bus scans (0070, 0075), which ran
+ * inside that same unpowered window. The module EEPROM at 0x50/0x58 kept
+ * answering throughout because it is fed from the always-on pm8008_l4 VIO
+ * rail, not from VANA.
  */
 / {
 	vreg_cam_front_vana: regulator-cam-front-vana {
@@ -1979,7 +1995,8 @@ vreg_cam_front_vana: regulator-cam-front-vana {
 		regulator-min-microvolt = <2856000>;
 		regulator-max-microvolt = <2856000>;
 		vin-supply = <&vreg_bob>;
-		gpio = <&tlmm 156 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+		gpio = <&tlmm 156 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		enable-active-high;
 	};
 };
 
